{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 4, :comments ["<b>In the month of the aviv.</b> The Torah fixed the festival of Pesach at the time of the aviv, which is the season of the barley harvest, for the stalk that has ripened is called aviv from the root av, because it is what gives birth to the grains of wheat. And it is known that all the appointed times of the Torah depend on seasons: the festival of Pesach at the season of the aviv, the festival of Shavuot at the season of the harvest, which is the wheat harvest, as it is said (Exodus 23:16): ״וחג הקציר בכורי מעשיך״, and the festival of Sukkot at the season of the ingathering. Therefore our Sages of blessed memory established for us in the prayer and in the blessings to say 'who sanctifies Israel and the seasons' (מקדש ישראל והזמנים), and not 'the appointed times' (המועדים) as in the language of Scripture, because all the appointed times depend on the season. And further, Nisan is called aviv because it is the father of the twelve months, and in it is the sign of the ram, which is father and firstborn of the twelve signs." "<b>By way of the Midrash.</b> ״בחדש האביב״ — a month that is fit for all, neither hot nor cold nor rainy. And so it says (Psalms 68:7): ״מוציא אסירים בכושרות״, in a fitting month. Rabbi Yochanan says: ״בכושרות״ — do not read בכושרות but rather בכי ושירות, 'with weeping and song': these weep and these sing. These weep, as it is said, ״כי אין בית אשר אין שם מת״; these sing, as it is said (Psalms 118:15): ״קול רנה וישועה באהלי צדיקים״."], :book :exodus, :voice :rabbeinu-bahya, :lang :en, :chapter 13, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T13:58:29.625552851Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}